.TH DRESSE 1 "2026-08-20" "realisateur" "bashified verb"
.SH NAME
dresse \- stand a self-dev host, or one account on it, up
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dresse
--host
[--check|--apply]
.br
.B dresse
--all
[--check|--apply]
.br
.B dresse
.I account
[--check|--apply]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Runs the provisioning steps that stand a self-dev host and its accounts up, in
order, idempotently. It is the front door for what were sixteen scripts a human
ran by hand out of a checkout on another machine.
.PP
It is typed by a human with
.BR sudo ,
on the host being provisioned. No self-dev account gets sudo, now or later; a
step that appears to need one is a wrong step.
.PP
.B --check
is the default and writes nothing. Because it writes nothing it runs as any
user, and says which rows will refuse for want of root.
.B --apply
must be root.
.SH THE PLAN
The steps are checked against
.BR bin/lib/propagation-set.sh :
every script a step runs must be provision-class there, and every
provision-class script
.B dresse
does not reach is printed as uncovered. A front door that quietly covers eleven
of sixteen reads exactly like one that covers sixteen.
.PP
Host-wide, in order: the GitHub App key
.RB ( selfdev-app-key.sh ),
the shared OAuth token
.RB ( selfdev-claude-token.sh ,
reported only \- it installs no secret unattended), the verb-build release
channel
.RB ( wire-release-channel.sh " --host)",
and the per-account
.B .claude
permissions and hooks blocks.
.PP
For an account that does not exist,
.BR setup-selfdev-project.sh
is itself the ordered sequence and is run whole. For one that does, only its
release bootstrap and its own clock are re-checked.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
every step ran, or under --check could.
.TP
.B 1
a step refused.
.TP
.B 2
usage error, or --apply without root.
.TP
.B 6
BLIND \- the propagation set could not be read, so the plan is unverifiable.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ausculte (1),
.BR discipline (1)
